From: Shirogane Tsumugi <artoriapendragon123@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] clk: sunxi-ng: suniv: constrain pll-video to the datasheet range

Experimenting with a 30 Hz mode for the hsd-nv3052 panel (812x1306 @
31.814 MHz) produced a real refresh rate of ~24.75 Hz, measured by
counting the TCON vblank interrupt over 12 s (297 irqs), while
clk_summary happily reported the promised rate.

Root cause chain:

  1. sun4i_dclk_round_rate() scans dividers 6..127 and takes the
     first divider whose parent clock request rounds *exactly*.
  2. For 31.814 MHz the first exact hit is div=43, which asks
     pll-video for 43 x 31.814 = 1368 MHz (24 MHz x 57, M=1) - a
     perfectly representable N/M combination, so the ccu math is
     happy to promise it.
  3. The F1C100s/200s datasheet (3.2.5.3 PLL_VIDEO Control Register)
     says: "The PLL output clock must be in the range of
     30MHz~600MHz." At 1368 MHz the PLL does not lock anywhere near
     the programmed rate and the pixel clock lands ~18% low.

Declare the datasheet range with the existing
SUNXI_CCU_NM_WITH_FRAC_GATE_LOCK_MIN_MAX macro (same thing A64 does
for its video PLLs). clk_round_rate() then refuses to promise
out-of-range rates and sun4i_dclk_round_rate() settles on an
achievable divider. pll-ve is left alone: the VE clock only ever
requests 432 MHz, already in range.

The panel keeps its stock 40 Hz raster: the NV3052 init sequence
programs the controller for the 812x1306 raster, and a mode with
different h/v totals (tried 800x1375 for an exact 30 Hz) loses sync
entirely. 42.418 MHz x 7 rounds to 297 MHz - the PLL fractional
sweet spot - so the 40 Hz mode is unaffected by the clamp; the clamp
just keeps any future mode experiment honest instead of silently
undershooting.

--- a/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-suniv-f1c100s.c
+++ b/drivers/clk/sunxi-ng/ccu-suniv-f1c100s.c
@@ -59,8 +59,14 @@
 				   BIT(28),		/* lock */
 				   CLK_SET_RATE_UNGATE);
 
-static SUNXI_CCU_NM_WITH_FRAC_GATE_LOCK(pll_video_clk, "pll-video",
+/* Datasheet 3.2.5.3: output must stay within 30MHz~600MHz. Without the
+ * clamp, sun4i_dclk_round_rate() happily locks onto exact N/M hits far
+ * above the VCO's reach (e.g. 1368 MHz) and the real pixel clock lands
+ * ~18% low. */
+static SUNXI_CCU_NM_WITH_FRAC_GATE_LOCK_MIN_MAX(pll_video_clk, "pll-video",
 					"osc24M", 0x010,
+					30000000,	/* min rate */
+					600000000,	/* max rate */
 					8, 7,		/* N */
 					0, 4,		/* M */
 					BIT(24),	/* frac enable */
